.SH NAME
taskstate \- a logical object definition in the xCAT database.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
\&\fBtaskstate Attributes:\fR  \fIcommand\fR, \fIdisable\fR, \fInode\fR, \fIpid\fR, \fIreserve\fR, \fIstate\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
Logical objects of this type are stored in the xCAT database in one or more tables.  Use the following commands
to manipulate the objects: \fBmkdef\fR, \fBchdef\fR, \fBlsdef\fR, and \fBrmdef\fR.  These commands will take care of
knowing which tables the object attributes should be stored in.  The attribute list below shows, in
parentheses, what tables each attribute is stored in.
.SH "taskstate Attributes:"
.IX Header "taskstate Attributes:"
.IP "\fBcommand\fR (taskstate.command)" 6
.IX Item "command (taskstate.command)"
Current command is running
.IP "\fBdisable\fR (taskstate.disable)" 6
.IX Item "disable (taskstate.disable)"
Set to 'yes' or '1' to comment out this row.
.IP "\fBnode\fR (taskstate.node)" 6
.IX Item "node (taskstate.node)"
The node name.
.IP "\fBpid\fR (taskstate.pid)" 6
.IX Item "pid (taskstate.pid)"
The process id of the request process.
.IP "\fBreserve\fR (taskstate.reserve)" 6
.IX Item "reserve (taskstate.reserve)"
used to lock the node
.IP "\fBstate\fR (taskstate.state)" 6
.IX Item "state (taskstate.state)"
The task state(callback, running) for the node.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IX Header "SEE ALSO"
\&\fBmkdef\|(1)\fR, \fBchdef\|(1)\fR, \fBlsdef\|(1)\fR, \fBrmdef\|(1)\fR
